==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[n-]]

===Etymology===
{{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*ne||not}}, from Proto-Indo-European {{term/t|ine-pro|*ne||no, not}}. Akin to {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|ne||not|lang=ang}}

===Prefix===
{{head|ang|prefix|cat=prefixes|head=ne-}}

# not, usually syncopated to n-
#: ''{{term|neom||am not|lang=ang}}''
#: ''{{term|næs||was not|lang=ang}}; {{term/t|ang|nǣron||were not}}''
#: ''{{term|nic||not I|lang=ang}}''
#: ''{{term/t|ang|nǣniġ||no one, none, not any, no (adj.)}}''
#: ''{{term|nealles||not at all, by no means|lang=ang}}''
#: ''{{term|nefne||unless, except, not even|lang=ang}}''
#: ''{{term|nabban||to have not, be without|lang=ang}}''
#: ''{{term/t|ang|nāgan||to not owe, not own}}''
#: ''{{term/t|ang|nǣfre||never}}''
#: ''{{term|nyllan||will not, wish not, be unwilling, refuse|lang=ang}}''
#: ''{{term/t|ang|nāt||[I] do not know}}, from {{term|nytan||to know not|lang=ang}}''
#: ''{{term|nyten||ignorant|lang=ang}} (from ne + witan)''

